  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report is in German and translates roughly to: 'Relatively speaking and in comparison to lottery A, the chance of losing money in lottery B is smaller, even though it's only at 55%. The probability of losing 3000€ is, however, very high at 90%. In lottery B, statistically speaking, more than every second outcome is 0.' The individual considers the probability of losing money in each lottery: they note that Lottery A has a 90% chance of a loss (very high), while Lottery B has only a 45% chance of loss (described as smaller, though they mention 55% — likely referring to the chance of no loss). They emphasize that in Lottery B, over half the outcomes are 0 (no loss), which is a favorable aspect. The justification appears to center on the higher likelihood of avoiding a loss in B, and the very high risk of loss in A. The individual seems to view avoiding a loss as important and evaluates the two lotteries in terms of the probability of any loss occurring, rather than the magnitude alone. There is no mention of 'skewness' or distributional shape; the focus is on comparative risk of loss and frequency of zero outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on right-skewness — preferring the lottery that assigns more probability to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., losses). That would be Lottery A (90% chance of loss), which the reason would favor. However, the individual’s reasoning emphasizes that Lottery B has a lower chance of loss and more frequent neutral outcomes (0€), which they seem to view positively. This suggests a preference for avoiding losses, which is the opposite of favoring right-skewness. The individual considers the same core information (probabilities of loss vs. no loss), so the data aligns. However, their evaluation rule — favoring lower probability of loss — contradicts the decision reason’s rule, which prefers more right-skewed (i.e., more loss-prone) lotteries. Therefore, while the individual considers relevant aspects (probabilities of outcomes), their justification opposes the decision reason’s logic. There is no indication they preferred A because it is more right-skewed; in fact, they seem to find the high loss probability in A problematic. Thus, it is certain they did not use this decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

