{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., has a higher probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones). To evaluate this, we assess the skewness of each lottery. Lottery A offers 500 Euros with 100% probability, which is a certain outcome and thus has zero variance and no skewness (symmetric by default). Lottery B offers 1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. This distribution is symmetric around its mean (500 Euros), meaning it is not skewed—neither right- nor left-skewed. Therefore, neither lottery is right-skewed, and the decision reason cannot differentiate between them based on skewness. The reason would be indifferent between A and B. However, the reason can still be applied in principle because skewness can be computed (or assessed qualitatively) from the given probabilities and outcomes.",
